  "response": "## The connectivity picture\n\nPulling the eight `high_fan_in_fan_out` findings together with the three `large_file` and the high-severity `large_function` hits:\n\n| File | fan-in | fan-out | transitive importers | LOC | biggest function |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/utils.ts` | **18** | 1 | 29 | **424** | — |\n| `src/plugin.ts` | **10** | 3 | 29 | — | `buildFinished` 103 |\n| `src/log.ts` | 9 | 0 | **30** | — | — |\n| `test/utils.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 | — | — |\n| `src/index.ts` | 4 | **17** | 7 | **459** | `build` 293 |\n| `src/esbuild/index.ts` | 1 | **11** | 7 | — | `runEsbuild` 237 |\n| `src/rollup.ts` | 0 | 7 | **0** | — | `getRollupConfig` 122 |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts` | 1 | 6 | 7 | — | `rollupDtsFiles` 85 |\n\nRepo cutoffs from the evidence lines: fan-in p95 = 9, p99 = 10; fan-out p95 = 6, p99 = 11.\n\nThe ordering principle: **fan-in is blast radius, fan-out is not.** A file with 18 importers propagates every change it makes to 18 modules. A file with 17 imports is an entry point — splitting it reduces nobody's exposure but its own. Both trip the same detector; only the first is a refactor priority.\n\n---\n\n## Refactor first\n\n### 1. `src/utils.ts` — fan-in 18, 424 lines (`crime_00086`, `crime_00048`, `crime_00087`, `crime_00010`)\n\nThe single highest-leverage target and it isn't close. Fan-in 18 is **1.8× the p99 cutoff of 10** — nothing else in the repo is more than 1× past it. 29 transitive importers, `blast_radius` 0.46, the top score in the scan.\n\nWhat makes it *safe* as well as high-value: there is **no `large_function` finding here.** It's a wide module, not a deep one — 424 lines of independent helpers, which is exactly the shape where a split-by-responsibility is mechanical. Keep a re-export shim at `src/utils.ts` and all 18 importers keep working while you move code out from under them.\n\nTwo findings that all 18 importers currently inherit and that a split would isolate:\n- `crime_00010` — 7 sync I/O calls across `remove